18. Junction: pseudopotential barrier, residual micromotion, peak versus rms
====================================================================================================
q phi_ps = 2.9e-5 eV  ->  E_0 = 1716.6 V/m   (brief 1.72e3 V/m)
z_1^peak = q E_0/(m Omega_rf^2) = 67.58 nm   (identity 2 sqrt(q phi_ps/(m Omega^2)) = 67.58 nm)
z_1^rms  = z_1^peak/sqrt(2)     = 47.78 nm   (the paper quotes 47 nm, the rms-field value)
  a code path returning 68 nm from the stated 2.9e-5 eV is correct in the PEAK convention, not buggy
static shift from the 8.7e-8 eV/um axial gradient in the 3.6 MHz well: 1.82 nm (rules that out as the explanation)

0.3 eV barrier for 9Be+ at 200 V peak, 2pi x 83 MHz requires |grad phi~_rf| = 873.0 m^-1 (brief 873 m^-1)
  peak axial rf field at the apex = V_rf |grad phi~_rf| = 1.746e+05 V/m  (brief 1.75e5)
  barrier rescaled as 1/m at fixed charge and drive: 40Ca+: 67.7 meV
  barrier rescaled as 1/m at fixed charge and drive: 171Yb+: 15.8 meV

====================================================================================================
19. Junction centre: the traceless-rf-Hessian identity omega_y = 2 omega_x
====================================================================================================
channel equivalence h_x = h_z = 1.0  ->  Laplace forces h_y = -2.0
omega_rf,i proportional to |h_i|  ->  omega_y/omega_x = 2.0000
with omega_x/2pi = 5.7 MHz this predicts omega_y/2pi = 11.4 MHz against the reported 11.3 MHz (ratio 1.982)
  the 5.7/5.7/11.3 MHz triple is self-consistent; the sum rule omega_x^2+omega_y^2+omega_z^2 = 2 omega_rf^2
  holds only where omega_rf,z = 0, i.e. in the straight channels and NOWHERE inside the junction
  a naive 2 omega_rf^2 reading at C would need omega_rf/2pi = 9.82 MHz, which the pseudopotential does not supply

